  "query_preview": "..A dry southwest flow aloft will continue for our area \nthrough Monday with weak troughing to our west/northwest and a ridge \nover the southern plains/Texas. Daytime temperatures will be a few \ndegrees warmer each day, with relatively light winds.\n\nTuesday through Thursday - an increase in moisture is forecast over\nthe eastern 1/3 of the state especially southern Apache and \nNavajo counties. This is in response to a brief period of \nsouth/southeast flow aloft as the weak trough approaches from the \nwest. Ensemble mean precipitable water values are in the \n0.50\"-0.60\" range over this area, peaking Wed/Thu. These values \nare down slightly from 24 hrs ago. Shower and thunderstorm chances\nare limited to around 20% for the White Mtns region Tuesday, \nincreasing to 30-60% Wed/Thu with 10-20% chances spreading into \nthe Chuska Mtns/Window Rock area. Elsewhere, the mid level \nmoisture increase should be enough for some afternoon high-based \ncumulus, but precipitation is not expected. Daytime temperatures \nwill be within a few degrees of normal, with typical early June \nwinds of 5-15 mph with gusts around 20 mph.\n\nFriday into next weekend - drier southwest flow is forecast to \nreturn as troughing develops again to our west. Increasing southwest \nwinds and a bit cooler temperatures could develop as the trough \napproaches toward the weekend.",
